# These patterns are anchored with a leading slash on purpose. An unanchored "artifacts/"
# matches a directory of that name at ANY depth, which would also exclude report/artifacts/ -
# the committed failure evidence. Git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded, so a later "!report/**" would not rescue it and the deliverable would silently
# go missing from the submission. Anchoring keeps the rules to the build output at the root.
/target/
/test-results/
/artifacts/

# allure-maven downloads the ~40 MB Allure commandline here on first report generation.
# It is a redownloadable tool, not source, so it must never enter the repository.
/.allure/

.idea/
.vscode/
*.log

# report/ is a tracked deliverable: the assessment asks for test reports and build artifacts
# to be present in the repository, so the generated Allure report, the TestNG report and the
# failure screenshots/videos live there rather than only in CI artifacts.
